meta {
  name: Presign Transfer Document
  type: http
  seq: 3
}

post {
  url: {{baseUrl}}/crm/service-tickets/{{ticketId}}/policy-transfer/presign
  body: json
  auth: inherit
}

headers {
  Content-Type: application/json
  ~X-Branch-Id: {{branchId}}
}

body:json {
  {
    "kind": "new_business_application",
    "filename": "application.pdf",
    "contentType": "application/pdf",
    "size": 2048
  }
}

script:post-response {
  if (res.getStatus() === 201) {
    bru.setVar("transferDocumentKey", res.getBody().key);
  }
}

tests {
  test("returns 201 Created", function () {
    expect(res.getStatus()).to.equal(201);
  });

  test("returns an upload URL and its key", function () {
    const body = res.getBody();
    expect(body.uploadUrl).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.key).to.be.a("string");
    expect(body.expiresIn).to.be.a("number");
  });

  test("keys the object under the household, not a lead", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().key).to.contain("/policy-transfers/");
  });

  test("puts a new business application under its own prefix", function () {
    expect(res.getBody().key).to.contain("/nba/");
  });
}

docs {
  # POST /crm/service-tickets/:id/policy-transfer/presign

  A presigned PUT for a document on an **in-progress** policy transfer — the
  household-anchored sibling of `POST /sold-deals/documents/presign`.

  - **Module:** `crm_service`
  - **Permission:** `crm_service:write`
  - **Data scope:** the ticket, exactly as on `…/policy-transfer`. The household
    is read off it server-side, so the body names no anchor at all.

  ## Why a separate endpoint

  The sold presign is keyed on a **lead**, which a transfer does not have. The
  key prefix is not cosmetic: `assertKeyOwnership` re-checks it at submit, so it
  *is* the ownership check. The two id spaces are kept disjoint —
  `sold-deals/{leadId}/…` and `policy-transfers/{householdId}/…` — precisely so
  a key presigned against one cannot be replayed against the other.

  ## Body

  | field | type | notes |
  |---|---|---|
  | `kind` | `discount_proof` \| `new_business_application` | Defaults to `discount_proof`. Decides both the allowed content types **and** the key prefix. |
  | `filename` | string, 1–255 | |
  | `contentType` | `application/pdf` \| `image/jpeg` \| `image/png` | **`new_business_application` is PDF-only** — it is a signed application, not a photographed receipt. |
  | `size` | int, ≤ 10MB | A *claim*, not evidence: the presigned PUT signs only the content type. `…/policy-transfer` re-derives the real size from storage with `HeadObject`, and that is what actually enforces the limit. |

  ## Upload flow

  1. `POST` here → `{ key, uploadUrl, requiredHeaders, expiresIn }`.
  2. `PUT` the bytes **straight to `uploadUrl`** with `requiredHeaders`. Use a
     bare fetch — an `Authorization` header invalidates the S3 signature. This
     is the single easiest thing to get wrong.
  3. Send `key` (plus filename/contentType/size) as the policy row's
     `newBusinessApplication` or discount-proof `attachment`.

  The bytes never pass through the API.

  ## Response `201`

  ```json
  {
    "key": "agencies/665f…/policy-transfers/665f…/nba/2026/application.pdf",
    "uploadUrl": "https://…",
    "requiredHeaders": { "Content-Type": "application/pdf" },
    "expiresIn": 900
  }
  ```

  ## Errors
  | Status | When |
  |---|---|
  | 400 | Content type not allowed for the `kind`; ticket has no linked household |
  | 401 | Missing/expired bearer token |
  | 403 | `crm_service` disabled, or the caller lacks `crm_service:write` |
  | 404 | No such ticket, or out of scope |
}
